
1. Launching Parallel Worktrees (Subagent Fanout)
Run up to 8 background agents simultaneously, each working inside its own isolated Git worktree so your active working directory stays completely untouched.



# Run multi-subagent refactor in isolated worktrees
muse "Refactor DB layer from Postgres to Prisma" \
  --parallel 4 \
  --isolation worktree \
  --auto-verify


How it works:

--parallel 4 spawns 4 background workers.


--isolation worktree creates temporary Git worktrees under .muse/worktrees/.


--auto-verify instructs the isolated Verifier Agent to run your unit tests in each worktree before signaling completion.


2. Stress-Testing an Implementation Plan (/grill)
Before letting the CLI edit any files, trigger adversarial plan review directly within an interactive session to catch edge cases early.



$ muse
> Create an in-memory rate limiter middleware for Express.
# [Muse generates an initial plan]

> /grill


How it works:

Entering /grill shifts the CLI from execution mode into adversarial review mode. The agent interrogates its own plan:

Plaintext
[Grill Output]
⚠️ Issue 1: In-memory counters fail in multi-instance load-balanced environments.
⚠️ Issue 2: High traffic bursts will cause unbounded memory usage.
💡 Recommendation: Switch to Redis sliding window algorithm. Update plan? (y/n)


3. Setting Up Enterprise OS Sandboxing (muse config)
Restrict terminal permissions and command execution boundaries to prevent unsafe shell executions on developer machines.

Ini, TOML
# .muse/config.toml
[sandbox]
mode = "strict"
allow_network = false
allowed_paths = ["./src", "./tests"]

[policy]
enforce_plane = "enterprise_default"
require_approval = ["npm install", "docker", "rm -rf"]


Validate configuration via CLI:



# Verify policy compliance against central security requirements
muse config validate --plane enterprise_default


How it works:

If a subagent attempts an unapproved action (like making an outbound HTTP call or modifying files outside ./src), the execution boundary intercepts the call and requires manual operator confirmation.

4. Configuring Team-Wide vs. Private Memory (AGENTS.md)
Set up repository-wide architectural rules for the entire team while keeping local user settings private.

Repository Rulefile (AGENTS.md - Committed to Git):

Markdown
# AGENTS.md - Repository Standards

## Database Operations
- Never use raw SQL queries; always use Prisma ORM methods.
- All new migrations must include down-migrations in `/prisma/migrations`.

## Testing Rules
- Every API endpoint addition requires an integration test in `/tests/api`.


Private Local Memory (.agents/memory/local.json - Git ignored):

JSON
{
  "preferred_editor": "cursor",
  "local_test_flag": "--reporter=nyan",
  "custom_sandbox_timeout": 30
}


How it works:

When any developer clones the repository and runs muse, the CLI automatically parses AGENTS.md into the Skill Recommendation & Observer Agents. This ensures AI-generated code follows team conventions automatically.

5. Inspecting Append-Only Session Replays (muse log)
Audit or replay session tool calls, file diffs, and executed shell commands step-by-step from the local event stream.

# View session history for the active branch
muse log --last 10 --format pretty

# Inspect exact tool calls made by a background subagent
muse log --subagent-id subagent_3a8b --diff


Sample Output:

Plaintext
[EVENT #104] Subagent-3a8b -> Executed Tool: 
Command: `npm test -- tests/auth.test.ts`
Status: PASSED (Exit code: 0)
Diff Applied: +24 lines, -8 lines in src/auth/jwt.ts


How it works:

Because sessions are recorded in an append-only event stream, if a session crashes or loses connection mid-step, running muse resume recovers the exact execution state without duplicating work.

